Action: prune_redundant  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool_full","corr_threshold":0.95,"metric":"rmse"}

Thought: The ranking is unstable and several models share correlated errors; a forward‑selection based weighting on the pruned pool4 may capture complementary strengths better than a simple mean on pool2.  
Action: weights_forward  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool4","max_steps":20,"metric":"rmse"}

Thought: The forward‑selection weights (w2) on the pruned pool4 pick three complementary models; evaluating this weighted combo may beat the current mean‑pool2 baseline.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"weighted","pool":"pool4","weights":"w2","rationale":"test forward‑selection weights on the pruned pool"}

Thought: The leader is a mean over pool2, but a robust median might reduce the influence of any outlier models and could beat the current best.  
Action: evaluate_strategy  
Action Input: {"combine":"median","pool":"pool2","rationale":"robust median on the stable pool"}

Thought: The current leader uses a robust mean on a stable pool; try a data‑driven weighting on the same pool to see if inverse‑error weights can beat the mean without overfitting.  
Action: weights_inverse_error  
Action Input: {"pool":"pool2","windows":null,"metric":"rmse","shrinkage":0.0,"per_horizon":false}

Thought: The leader a6 (mean on pool2) is a seed strategy and the diagnostics say the margin is indistinguishable; the series is trend‑dominated and robust combinations are preferred, so accepting a6 is justified.  
Action: accept  
Action Input: {"attempt_id":"a6","confidence":"high","justification":"mean pool2 provides a robust, low‑variance forecast for a trend‑dominated series and is statistically indistinguishable from the runner‑up."}

